faqs:
- q: How do I help my elderly parent move house?
  a: Plan early, involve them in every decision, sort belongings room by room at their pace, and have one person coordinate the packing, removal and settling in. Chayce provides one coordinator for the whole move, so the family is not managing several companies.
- q: Can you manage the move if I live far away?
  a: Yes. Your dedicated coordinator handles the work on the ground and keeps you updated by phone or email. See <a href="/blog/helping-a-parent-move-home-when-you-live-far-away-uk/">helping a parent move when you live far away</a>.
- q: How do I start the conversation about moving with my parent?
  a: Begin with what they want from their next home rather than what the family has decided. Listen first, involve them in each decision and agree a timeline together. A Discovery Consultation can include your parent and family in building the plan.
- q: What if my elderly parent refuses to move?
  a: Do not push. Ask what worries them, listen without arguing, and explore smaller steps such as visiting a place or making changes at home. See our guide on <a href="/blog/how-to-talk-to-elderly-parents-about-moving/">how to talk to elderly parents about moving</a>.
- q: When is it time for an elderly parent to move?
  a: There is no single sign. Common prompts are a home that has become hard to manage, difficulty with stairs or upkeep, isolation or safety worries. A GP, an occupational therapist or your council can help you judge what might suit.
- q: Can you help with moving a parent into a care home or nursing home?
  a: 'Yes, with the practical side: choosing what comes, packing, the removal and setting up the room. The decision and the choice of home stay with your parent, the family and their professionals. See <a href="/care-home-removals/">care home removals</a>.'
- q: Can you help if my parent has dementia?
  a: We work at a gentle pace, keep familiar things together and involve the family throughout. We do not give care or medical advice. Our guide to <a href="/blog/moving-house-with-dementia-in-the-family/">moving house with dementia in the family</a> may help.
- q: Can you work with my parent directly and keep the family updated separately?
  a: Yes. We work with the person who is moving and keep family members informed to whatever degree suits your family.
